Skill "code-review" completed (forked execution).

Result:
```json
[
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/strings.json",
    "line": 18,
    "summary": "(a) The reworded slots_already_configured text tells the user to free the numbers by removing the users holding them from either configuration. Removing a user and adding them back hands out the same number again, so the conflict comes straight back.",
    "failure_scenario": "Entry A holds slot 1 and entry B manages slot 1 on lock M. A's options flow refuses M. Following the advice, the user removes A's user and adds them back right away. A does not hold M yet, so `get_managed_slots` (claimed_by_other_entries) does not count B's numbers on M, and the occupancy read on A's own locks shows 1 as free. A number an entry just released can be reused (see test_an_entry_may_reuse_a_number_it_just_released), so the user gets slot 1 again and adding M still fails. Doing it from B's side fails the same way. The only order that works is: remove the users, add the lock, then add the users back. The text never gives that order, so the user goes round in a loop. The same text is at line 151 (options) and in translations/en.json."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/config_flow.py",
    "line": 765,
    "summary": "(a) In a lock swap, the new 'newly added only' rule lets the first entry through and blocks the second, even though the overlap they share already existed.",
    "failure_scenario": "Entries A and B both hold lock L and already overlap on slots 1-2. L is re-included as L2, so both entries fail setup and start reauth. A's reauth adds L2; B does not hold L2 yet, so it passes. B's reauth then adds L2, A now holds it with numbers 1-2, and B gets slots_already_configured. B can only recover by removing users. That contradicts the stated rule that an overlap the entry already carries must not block reauth, and which entry gets stuck depends only on which reauth the user finishes first."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/config_flow.py",
    "line": 761,
    "summary": "(c) Reauth accepts a submission that still contains the missing lock. `_newly_added` (and before it, the mqtt and capacity checks) passes it through, so the entry reloads, fails, and opens reauth again.",
    "failure_scenario": "Reauth is started for missing `lock.gone`. The user submits [lock.gone, lock.x], perhaps just accepting the pre-filled default. No check rejects lock.gone. The flow aborts with 'locks_updated' and reloads. async_setup_entry finds lock.gone missing again and raises ConfigEntryError plus a new reauth. The user sees a success message and then the same repair prompt again, with no error that says why."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/config_flow.py",
    "line": 878,
    "summary": "(c) The new overlap check looks at other entries' saved config only at submit time. An allocation running at the same time in another entry's config flow or user subentry flow can still commit overlapping numbers, and nothing checks again at save.",
    "failure_scenario": "Entry B's 'add user' subentry flow starts an allocation progress task. It reads B's locks, which include M, and works out which numbers are claimed while entry A does not hold M yet. A slow lock read can take minutes. Meanwhile A's options flow adds M. B's saved numbers do not overlap A's, so the check passes. B's allocation then finishes and saves a number A already holds, for example 2, and now both entries manage slot 2 on M. The reverse case is the same: B's allocation snapshot misses A's in-flight user. The window is as long as the progress read."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/config_flow.py",
    "line": 206,
    "summary": "(c) An overlap that already exists, which options and reauth now deliberately leave alone, is never reported anywhere. Both entries keep writing the same slot, and lookups by lock and slot assume only one entry owns it.",
    "failure_scenario": "Two entries manage slot 1 on lock M; the options flow allowed this before this branch. Every edit now succeeds with no error and no repair is raised. Each entry's sync writes its own PIN to slot 1 on M and overwrites the other's, so in-sync status flaps. `find_entry_for_lock_slot` (domain/queries.py:88) returns whichever entry comes first, so code-used events are credited to the wrong user. The user has no indication of why."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/config_flow.py",
    "line": 872,
    "summary": "(a) Options and reauth now repeat the same three-check sequence: unclaimed mqtt, then common slots, then capacity on `_newly_added`. The copies already differ slightly; options skips capacity when there are no slot numbers, reauth does not.",
    "failure_scenario": "About 25 lines of check-and-overwrite logic are duplicated at lines 761-783 and 872-899, each with its own errors/placeholders handling. The next check added to one flow will be missed in the other, which is how the options flow lost its capacity check before (#1514). One helper, e.g. `_async_check_added_locks(hass, entry, submitted, held, slots) -> (errors, placeholders)`, would do the job for both."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/config_flow.py",
    "line": 135,
    "summary": "(a) `_newly_added` is recomputed up to three times per submission. Each helper takes an `already_configured` argument only to re-derive the same list, and each docstring re-explains the rule.",
    "failure_scenario": "Computing `added = _newly_added(submitted, config.locks)` once in each flow and passing `added` to `_check_unclaimed_mqtt_locks`, `_check_common_slots` and `async_check_slot_capacity` would remove the extra `already_configured` parameter from two helpers and the three copies of the docstring. It would also make the rule impossible to skip at one call site while the others apply it."
  }
]
```

