toolUseResult.result:
I found one finding that a user can actually see, plus two lower ones. Each summary is tagged (a) introduced by this branch or (c) predates it. I dropped cleanup-only items because you asked for observable behavior or clear correctness defects only. Those were: the `out_of_range_slots` placeholder that no string uses any more but tests still assert, the unreachable `config_subentries` `slot_out_of_range` string, and `slot_out_of_range` copied word for word into three sections.

```json
[
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/strings.json",
    "line": 18,
    "summary": "(a; the advice itself predates in substance) config.error.slot_out_of_range is only shown by reauth, where this configuration is by definition not loaded, but the rewritten text tells the user to 'remove those users, or to manage them from a separate configuration'. That breaks the branch's own rule that text sends users only to a loaded configuration, which config.error.slots_already_configured right below it follows.",
    "failure_scenario": "Entry E fails setup because lock A was removed, so reauth opens. The user picks replacement lock R, which reports 1 slot while 'Bob (2)' holds slot 2, and gets slot_out_of_range. Following the text, they delete Bob's user subentry while E is unloaded. The update listener (__init__.py:936) is only registered while E is loaded, so nothing clears Bob's PIN from E's remaining locks. Setup then diffs against EntryConfig.empty() (__init__.py:948), so the next load does not clear it either, and the deleted user's PIN keeps opening E's other locks (the #1453 class of bug). options.error.slot_out_of_range (line 151) gives the same advice when the options flow runs on an unloaded entry."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/domain/allocation.py",
    "line": 187,
    "summary": "(a) out_of_range_users is filled in inside the shared domain capacity check for every refusal. Three callers pass a count, not a slot number: [num_users] at line 443, [wider] at line 501, and [wanted] at config_flow.py line 345. For those, describe_holders names whichever user happens to hold the slot whose number equals the count.",
    "failure_scenario": "An entry with 5 users, where 'Carol' holds slot 5, adds a block of users that will not fit. The SlotAllocationError that is re-raised as too_many_users (or numbers_needed_exceed_capacity) carries out_of_range_users='Carol (5)', a user unrelated to the refusal. No string renders it today, so the placeholders are wrong but latent. Any future string or log that uses them would blame Carol. Building the name in the config_flow except-branch of _async_check_submitted_locks, which holds the real slot numbers, would keep presentation out of allocation and avoid the mislabel."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/domain/config.py",
    "line": 301,
    "summary": "(a) describe_holders appends ' (N)' to a name the user chose, so a name that contains parentheses produces exactly the nested parentheses the branch's plain-text rule forbids. The test only checks the rule against fixture names that have none.",
    "failure_scenario": "A user named 'Mom (old phone)' who holds slot 3 overlaps on a lock being added. The slots_already_configured error then reads 'In this configuration: Mom (old phone) (3)', which is the nested-parentheses form the regex in the new test rejects. The test passes only because its own fixture names contain no parentheses."
  }
]
```

