toolUseResult.result:
I reviewed a9aee1f8 against origin/main. The worktree was not modified; I ran tests and mutations in a scratch copy under /tmp and deleted it afterwards.

The matching logic does what the commit says, and the tests catch it breaking. With main's `zha.py` swapped in, 18 of the 36 new e2e tests fail. Each targeted mutation also fails the suite: the stock-class `isinstance` check, dropping the `user_id` check, dropping `command_id == request.id`, dropping the int guard, dropping the `user_status` guard, dropping the bytes decoding, and nulling the own-response status. The full ZHA suite plus the property tests pass on the branch (187 passed, 1 skipped). The only mutation that survived is equivalent: no Door Lock cluster command has a `command_id` field.

What's left is about where a write ends up when its reply is identified as not its own:

```json
[
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/zha.py",
    "line": 411,
    "summary": "A clear whose reply is recognized as foreign (e.g. a Default Response FAILURE naming lock_door, or the set's response) now returns True and pushes SlotCredential.empty(), which the base treats as the lock's own word on a push provider; on main this case raised CodeRejectedError and kept the code known.",
    "failure_scenario": "A user is removed and LCM sends clear_pin_code. A Default Response(lock_door, FAILURE) with the same sequence number fills the pending clear, and the lock's real clear response, a refusal, arrives unmatched and is dropped. _write_status returns None, so the clear 'stands': the slot is pushed as empty, in-sync goes ON, and allocation sees the slot as free. With programming events supported, hard_refresh_interval is None, so nothing ever reads it back and the deleted user's PIN keeps opening the door (the #1453 failure class)."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/zha.py",
    "line": 378,
    "summary": "A set whose reply is recognized as not being its answer is reported WriteResult.CONFIRMED and pushes known(pin); on a push provider the base then calls drop_pending, so the write is never verified. WriteResult.OPTIMISTIC exists for exactly this 'treated as done but not confirmed' case and would make the coordinator read the slot back.",
    "failure_scenario": "set_pin_code on slot 1 is filled by an operation_event_notification or a clear_pin_code_response FAILURE that shares its sequence number, and the lock's real set_pin_code_response (status 1, general failure) is dropped. The provider returns CONFIRMED with known(pin), in-sync reads ON, and no read-back is scheduled, so the slot shows in sync while the lock does not hold the code. On main a status-bearing foreign frame raised; the fix moves these frames from 'wrongly rejected' to 'wrongly confirmed, never checked' instead of 'unconfirmed, read back'."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/providers/zha/test_e2e.py",
    "line": 122,
    "summary": "Every e2e test of a foreign reply filling a write uses _applied, so the lock really performs the set or clear; none covers a foreign frame filling a write the lock actually refused, which is the case where the new 'leave it standing' fallback matters.",
    "failure_scenario": "Swap _applied for an answer that does not change door_lock.codes but returns default_response(lock_door, FAILURE). The slot then reads in sync ON while door_lock.codes lacks the PIN, and no assertion in the suite catches it. The tests prove the fix stops false rejections, not what the fallback does when the write did not land."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/providers/zha/conftest.py",
    "line": 585,
    "summary": "The e2e stand-in replaces endpoint.request, which sits above zigpy's Device.request and _maybe_match_response, so the defect being fixed (zigpy pairing a different frame to a pending request by sequence number) is never exercised; the test chooses what the request returns.",
    "failure_scenario": "zigpy behavior the provider depends on, such as which cluster decodes the matched frame (_find_zcl_cluster), the Report_Attributes exclusion, or a ParsingError delivered as an exception, could change and every test would still pass. A test that registers a pending request and feeds a colliding frame through device.packet_received would check the actual input contract instead of the test's own model of it."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/providers/zha/test_provider.py",
    "line": 489,
    "summary": "test_an_own_response_with_no_numeric_status_is_no_answer asserts `in (WriteResult.CONFIRMED, True)` for both commands and `push_update.assert_called_once()` without checking the value, so a wrong return type or wrong pushed credential would pass.",
    "failure_scenario": "If async_set_credential returned True, or pushed SlotCredential.empty() for a set (or known() for a clear), on these quirk replies, the test would still pass, because True is in the accepted tuple and the pushed value is never asserted."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/providers/zha/test_provider.py",
    "line": 397,
    "summary": "The new write tests replace the coordinator with a MagicMock even though the simple_lcm_config_entry fixture provides a real one; this breaks the AGENTS.md test convention, which /Users/raman/projects/lock_code_manager/CLAUDE.md names as the master guidance.",
    "failure_scenario": "AGENTS.md 'Test conventions' says: \"Don't use `MagicMock()` for coordinators when the real coordinator can be used.\" New tests at lines 397, 435 and 487 set `zha_lock.coordinator = MagicMock()`, so they only check that push_update was called, not what the real coordinator does with a no-answer write. That is exactly the downstream behavior in the first two findings."
  }
]
```

