toolUseResult.result:
I reviewed the fix (a9aee1f8 and df74592e) and found 6 issues. Two are clear: on main the "no usable status" case was rejected, not confirmed as claimed, and the end-to-end tests can't tell `LockOperationFailed` from `LockDisconnected`. The other four are plausible or cleanup-level.

On a scratch copy, the ZHA provider and property tests pass (229 passed, 1 skipped). I also ran one mutation there: changing the foreign-reply raise from `LockOperationFailed` to `LockDisconnected` left all end-to-end tests green, and only the property test failed. The worktree is unchanged and the scratch copy has been removed.

```json
[
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/zha.py",
    "line": 104,
    "summary": "The fix claims an own response with no usable status 'keeps main's behavior (confirmed + push)', but main rejected two of the three shapes the tests cover; the new code confirms and pushes them.",
    "failure_scenario": "Main's check was `hasattr(result, \"status\") and result.status != 0`. I checked it against zigpy: an optional `status?` that is absent decodes to `status=None`, and `None != 0` is True, so main raised CodeRejectedError. A quirk that decodes status as `LVBytes(b\"\\x01\")` also gives `!= 0` True, so main rejected that too. The new `_write_status` returns None for both, so the write is CONFIRMED and pushed as known or empty. A refusal from a quirk whose status is not a number now reads as in sync while the code never landed, which breaks df74592e's own rule that an unknown outcome fails and is re-sent. `test_an_own_response_with_no_numeric_status_is_no_answer` (test_provider.py:533) asserts the new behavior, so nothing records the change from main. Only the 'named_otherwise' shape actually matches main."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/providers/zha/test_e2e.py",
    "line": 346,
    "summary": "The end-to-end 'is sent again' tests can't tell LockOperationFailed (slot breaker) from LockDisconnected (lock breaker), so the claim that 'the slot breaker counts it' is not proven end to end.",
    "failure_scenario": "Changing `raise LockOperationFailed(` at zha.py:98 to `raise LockDisconnected(` leaves every test in test_e2e.py green; only the property test fails. Under that mutant, three foreign replies would trip the LOCK breaker and mark the whole lock unreachable instead of suspending one slot. The tests only assert '>= 2 sends, not in sync, still enabled', which both exception types produce. No test drives repeated foreign replies until the slot breaker suspends the slot."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/zha.py",
    "line": 377,
    "summary": "A reply zigpy fails to parse ends up as LockDisconnected, not LockOperationFailed, so 'any other reply raises LockOperationFailed' does not hold for that kind of reply.",
    "failure_scenario": "In zigpy, `packet_received` puts a ParsingError on the pending future when a frame on the write's sequence number fails to deserialize, for example a truncated event or a quirk's mis-sized frame. `Device.request` re-raises ParsingError without retrying. `async_set_credential` / `async_delete_credential` catch it with `except Exception` and raise LockDisconnected. That charges the lock connectivity breaker, so three such frames mark the lock unreachable. Undecoded raw bytes, by contrast, correctly raise LockOperationFailed. The `except Exception` routing comes from main, but the new contract leaves this kind of reply out."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/zha.py",
    "line": 421,
    "summary": "A clear that fails on a foreign reply during slot release is never retried, because no sync owns a released slot; 'sync re-sends' does not cover this path.",
    "failure_scenario": "A user is deleted and `async_release_managed_slot` runs `async_internal_clear_usercode`. The lock refuses the clear, but an operation event fills the request, so LockOperationFailed is raised. `__init__.py:2124` logs a warning and moves on. The slot is no longer managed, so nothing sends the clear again and the deleted user's PIN keeps opening the door (the #1453 class of bug). This is no worse than main (which falsely confirmed), but the commit's stated remedy (re-send) does not apply here and no test covers the release path."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/zha.py",
    "line": 385,
    "summary": "After a foreign reply, the set is sent again with no read-back first, so a set that already landed is written a second time.",
    "failure_scenario": "The lock applies set_pin_code, but an event with the same sequence number fills the request, so LockOperationFailed is raised and nothing is pushed. On the next tick the coordinator still holds the old value, so sync sends the identical set again without reading the slot. Whether the lock answers a repeat write of the code it already holds with a non-zero status is unverified; if it does, `_write_status` returns that status and CodeRejectedError disables the slot over a code that was set correctly. The end-to-end test's stand-in (`_applied_once`) always accepts the repeat, so this case isn't exercised."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/providers/zha.py",
    "line": 69,
    "summary": "`_write_status` takes `code_slot` and `lock_entity_id` only to format an error message, and the status → CodeRejectedError block is repeated in the set and clear paths.",
    "failure_scenario": "Six positional arguments, two of them used only for message text, and two near-identical `if status := _write_status(...): raise CodeRejectedError(...)` blocks at lines 385 and 421. A single helper that raises both LockOperationFailed and CodeRejectedError, taking the request name, would remove the copy-paste and keep the two writes' policies from drifting apart."
  }
]
```

