toolUseResult.result:
I found 5 defects in this fix. The first one is a data-loss regression, and I reproduced it in a scratch copy made with `git archive` (now deleted). The worktree was not touched. On the branch, `tests/test_config_flow.py` and `tests/test_init.py` pass: 206 passed.

```json
[
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/config_flow.py",
    "line": 889,
    "summary": "The options flow now sends every non-LOADED entry through _async_save_locks_and_reload, which writes data=EntryConfig.to_dict() and options={}; to_dict() keeps only extra + locks, so an entry that has not been migrated loses its users (still stored under CONF_SLOTS/CONF_USERS in data) for good.",
    "failure_scenario": "Reproduced in a scratch copy. A version-4 entry with data={locks:[lock.test_1], slots:{'1':{name:'Alice',pin:'1234',enabled:True}}} and state MIGRATION_ERROR (or NOT_LOADED and never migrated, e.g. disabled); the options flow is submitted with the same locks. Branch result: abort 'locks_updated', data={'locks':['lock.test_1']}, options={}, users={}, and the reload's migration logs 'moved 0 user(s) into subentries'. On origin/main the same test leaves data.slots in place and keeps Alice. Reauth never reached un-migrated entries (it only starts from async_setup_entry, after migration), so this exposure is new with the options path."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/config_flow.py",
    "line": 236,
    "summary": "`is not LOADED` also matches states Home Assistant will not reload (MIGRATION_ERROR, FAILED_UNLOAD). In those states the background async_reload raises OperationNotAllowed, yet the flow still reports success: the same false-success this fix exists to remove.",
    "failure_scenario": "An entry is in FAILED_UNLOAD (its unload_entry raised) or MIGRATION_ERROR while the domain is loaded for other entries. The user saves options: dropped locks are released, data is rewritten, options are cleared and the flow aborts with 'locks_updated'. Then ConfigEntries.async_reload -> async_unload hits `if not entry.state.recoverable: raise OperationNotAllowed`, which is logged only as an unhandled task exception. The entry stays failed, and the user was told it was updated."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/config_flow.py",
    "line": 889,
    "summary": "The branch condition uses entry state as a stand-in for 'no update listener', and the two do not line up. The listener is added only in _setup_entry_after_start (after EVENT_HOMEASSISTANT_STARTED), so a LOADED entry during startup has no listener, while the non-LOADED set also takes in transient and non-recoverable states.",
    "failure_scenario": "Saved during startup (entry LOADED, Home Assistant not yet started): the save goes to options with no listener and depends on the post-start initial pass to apply it. Saved during SETUP_IN_PROGRESS or UNLOAD_IN_PROGRESS: it is forced through release+rewrite+reload. The precise predicate is `not self.config_entry.update_listeners` (what Home Assistant itself checks for OptionsFlowWithReload), or an explicit set of recoverable failed states (SETUP_ERROR, SETUP_RETRY), not `is not LOADED`."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/test_init.py",
    "line": 2378,
    "summary": "The only test of the new path uses SETUP_ERROR on an already-migrated entry. Nothing tests the other states the `is not LOADED` branch now captures, and nothing asserts that users or subentries survive the rewrite, so findings 1 and 2 pass the suite unseen.",
    "failure_scenario": "Change the predicate to anything broader, or let the data write drop legacy CONF_SLOTS/CONF_USERS: every test still passes. A test with an un-migrated or MIGRATION_ERROR entry that checks get_entry_config(entry).users after the save would fail on this branch."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/config_flow.py",
    "line": 236,
    "summary": "The shared helper reloads via hass.async_create_task(async_reload(...)) instead of hass.config_entries.async_schedule_reload, which cancels any pending setup retry before it schedules. The options path now reaches retrying entries, which reauth never did.",
    "failure_scenario": "An entry in SETUP_RETRY gets an options save: the reload task is only scheduled, so the pending retry can fire before async_reload's own async_cancel_retry_setup runs, and setup races the reload. Home Assistant's docstring on async_reload recommends async_schedule_reload for exactly this reason."
  }
]
```

