content.content:
Skill "code-review" completed (forked execution).

Result:
I reviewed commit 8700a9ab against origin/main and found 10 defects. I ran the probe tests in a scratch copy of the commit and deleted it afterwards; the worktree was not modified. The harness's own `tests/test_harness.py` passes (32 tests). My probe tests confirmed findings 1, 3 and 4. The rest come from reading the code: 2 and 7 follow directly from it, while 5, 6 and 8 are plausible but not reproduced.

```json
[
  {
    "file": "tests/common.py",
    "line": 871,
    "summary": "`MockLCMLock.clear_faults()` only clears faults still in `self.faults`, but `_meet_fault` already removed any fault whose `times` ran out on its first hit, so a held `hang`/`drop_reply` with `times=N` is never released by it.",
    "failure_scenario": "`f = lock.hang(\"set\", 1, times=1)`; a write is held (`f` is dropped from `lock.faults` on that hit); `lock.clear_faults()`; `await write` hangs forever. Confirmed in scratch: the task is still pending after run-until-quiet. Only `f.clear()` or the end-of-test `release_all_faults()` lets it go, and the docstring says clear_faults releases 'any call one holds'."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/common.py",
    "line": 850,
    "summary": "The `hang` docstring tells tests to `await fault.entered.wait()` to know a call is held. That wait has no bound, so it hangs instead of failing, which is the exact problem `async_wait_held` exists to avoid.",
    "failure_scenario": "A test installs `hang(\"set\", 1)` on a slot that is already in sync (or forgets to step the clock), then follows the docstring with `await fault.entered.wait()`. Nothing ever enters, the frozen clock never fires a tick, and the test hangs until pytest-timeout instead of failing with 'no set held'."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/common.py",
    "line": 698,
    "summary": "`async_wait_held` checks `entered`, which is set on the first hold and never reset, so after a held call is released (or for a second call on a `times=None` fault) it passes while nothing is held.",
    "failure_scenario": "`f = lock.hang(\"set\", 1)`; the first write is held, then released by `f._released`/the sync path. A later `await f.async_wait_held()` meant to assert a SECOND write is in flight still passes (confirmed in scratch), so a regression test asserting 'the retry is held at the lock' passes vacuously."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/common.py",
    "line": 884,
    "summary": "`_meet_fault` counts a hit and spends `times` on every matching call, even ones the fault cannot affect: an `ignore` on a set that is already NO_CHANGE, or an ignore on a clear of an empty slot.",
    "failure_scenario": "`f = lock.ignore(\"set\", 1, times=1)`; the sync's first set to slot 1 writes the value already held (NO_CHANGE), which uses up the fault; the next real set applies. Confirmed in scratch: `codes[1]` changes. The test thinks it injected an ignored write but never did, and `f.hits` reports 1."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/conftest.py",
    "line": 76,
    "summary": "Faults are released in a `pytest_runtest_call` wrapper, which never runs when setup fails or errors. A fault a fixture installed during setup stays held through fixture teardown and stays in the module-level `_INSTALLED_FAULTS` into the next test.",
    "failure_scenario": "A fixture installs `hang(\"set\",1)` and advances a tick so a sync write is held, then a later fixture errors. `runtest_call` is skipped, the entry unload waits on the held tick on a frozen clock, and teardown hangs. The leftover fault is then cleared in the next test's hook, whose `Event.set()` resolves a waiter future from the previous test's closed loop and can raise 'Event loop is closed'. Putting the hook on `pytest_runtest_teardown` (before finalizers) covers both cases."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/common.py",
    "line": 333,
    "summary": "`async_advance_sync_ticks` jumps the clock 2.003 s in one `freezer.tick`, so every timer due in that span (confirmation reads, pending-write deadlines, coordinator poll) fires at the end time in the same loop pass as the sync tick. Their work then runs interleaved with the tick instead of finishing earlier, as it would in production.",
    "failure_scenario": "A confirmation read is scheduled 0.5 s after a write, and the next tick is due at 2 s. In production the read lands and updates coordinator data about 1.5 s before the tick. Here both callbacks are popped in the same `_run_once`, the read task and the tick task start together, and the read sees `time.monotonic()` already 2.003 s later. A regression test can then observe a tick racing a read that cannot race in reality, giving a false failure or a false pass. Stepping to each next scheduled handle's due time would preserve the ordering."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/common.py",
    "line": 428,
    "summary": "`async_trip_slot_breaker` catches every `pytest.fail.Exception` from `async_tick_until`, including the one from `async_settle_background` timing out, and replaces it with 'not suspended … was it out of sync before the first tick?'.",
    "failure_scenario": "Background work never settles (for example, another lock's held call or a task stuck behind it). The settle failure that names 'background lock work' is swallowed and replaced with a misleading out-of-sync message, sending the debugger to the wrong cause."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/common.py",
    "line": 991,
    "summary": "`async_get_usercodes` now computes `scope` from `managed_slots` before a `hang`/`drop_reply` holds the read, so a released read answers for the managed slots as they were before the hold, not at answer time.",
    "failure_scenario": "`hang(\"get\")` holds a poll; meanwhile a user is added and slot 3 becomes managed; on release the result omits empty slot 3. A real provider walks the slots when it reads, so the mock diverges, and the pending-write machinery that waits on a read covering the slot sees no answer for it."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/common.py",
    "line": 943,
    "summary": "`run_times` is appended before `_meet_fault`, so a `fail`/`refuse` call that 'never reached the lock' (per the knob docs) is still recorded as a lock operation.",
    "failure_scenario": "A pacing or regression test combines `lock.fail(\"set\", 1, times=1)` with `_assert_paced(lock.run_times, ...)`. The rejected attempt appears as an operation timestamp, so gap assertions are off by one entry and fail (or pass) for the wrong reason."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/common.py",
    "line": 735,
    "summary": "The `hang`/`drop_reply` hold (an `entered` event plus a release event) re-implements the existing `async_blocking_stub` in tests/common.py, which already provides `(stub, entered, release)`. There are now two hold mechanisms whose semantics differ, and only one of them is covered by `release_all_faults`.",
    "failure_scenario": "The 18 existing `async_blocking_stub` users are not released by the new teardown hook, so a later test using the old stub on a frozen clock still hangs teardown. Maintainers must keep two hold primitives in sync. Either have `LockFault` reuse the stub's events or register stub releases with `_INSTALLED_FAULTS`."
  }
]
```

