    "file": "tests/common.py",
    "line": 333,
    "summary": "`async_advance_sync_ticks` jumps the clock 2.003 s in one `freezer.tick`, so every timer due in that span (confirmation reads, pending-write deadlines, coordinator poll) fires at the end time in the same loop pass as the sync tick. Their work then runs interleaved with the tick instead of finishing earlier, as it would in production.",
    "failure_scenario": "A confirmation read is scheduled 0.5 s after a write, and the next tick is due at 2 s. In production the read lands and updates coordinator data about 1.5 s before the tick. Here both callbacks are popped in the same `_run_once`, the read task and the tick task start together, and the read sees `time.monotonic()` already 2.003 s later. A regression test can then observe a tick racing a read that cannot race in reality, giving a false failure or a false pass. Stepping to each next scheduled handle's due time would preserve the ordering."
  },
