Skill "code-review" completed (forked execution).

Result:
```json
[
  {
    "file": "tests/common.py",
    "line": 363,
    "summary": "The new per-timer stepping fires a sync manager's interval tick twice in one async_advance_sync_ticks call when that tick is overdue or due within CLOCK_STEP of the start. It fires on time, HA re-arms it at fire-time + 2 s, and that is still before end = start + TICK_INTERVAL + CLOCK_STEP. The old single jump fired it once and re-armed it past the end.",
    "failure_scenario": "Reproduced in a scratch copy. After one advance, call freezer.tick(1.996) or freezer.tick(1.9995), then async_advance_sync_ticks(hass, freezer). Each of the 4 _TrackTimeInterval(2s) timers fires 2 times instead of 1. With no clock movement in between, the tick's offset drifts down by CLOCK_STEP per call, and call #666 of a plain loop double-ticks. Any async_settle_background, async_run_paced or freezer.tick between calls speeds up the drift. The result is two failed writes charged in one 'tick', wrong counts from async_tick_until / _async_tick_until, and the breaker tripping in fewer calls than MAX_SYNC_ATTEMPTS, which breaks the docstring's 'fires once' and 'each failure on a tick of its own' guarantees."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/common.py",
    "line": 959,
    "summary": "clear_faults now iterates the module-level _INSTALLED_FAULTS set (ordered by id hash) instead of the lock's install-ordered faults list, so held calls are released in arbitrary order.",
    "failure_scenario": "Reproduced. Hang set on slots 5..10, start one write per slot, call lock.clear_faults(). service_calls['set_usercode'] came back in the order [10, 8, 5, 6, 9, 7], wrong in 50 of 50 trials. Before this commit it was [5..10]. Any test that holds several calls and asserts the order of what reached the lock after clear_faults becomes memory-layout dependent and flaky."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/common.py",
    "line": 416,
    "summary": "The tick count from _async_tick_until / async_tick_until is no longer 'sync ticks taken', because one async_advance_sync_ticks call can contain two scheduled ticks (see finding 1).",
    "failure_scenario": "A test asserts `taken == N`, or async_trip_slot_breaker budgets MAX_SYNC_ATTEMPTS + 2 calls, after a previous step left the tick within 3 ms of the next start. The breaker suspends after about half the calls and the returned count is off by the number of double ticks. Nothing reports the mismatch."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/test_harness.py",
    "line": 113,
    "summary": "No harness test pins 'exactly one scheduled tick per call'. test_advancing_sync_ticks_moves_both_clocks only asserts clock deltas with >=, and the new timer test only checks a one-shot timer, so the double-tick regression passes the suite.",
    "failure_scenario": "Finding 1 ships green: 237 tests pass across test_harness, test_sync and providers/test_base, while an interval timer due early in the span fires twice per call. A test that counts _TrackTimeInterval firings after freezer.tick(TICK_INTERVAL - 0.001) would catch it."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/common.py",
    "line": 364,
    "summary": "Off-by-one in the for/else timer cap: the `now >= end` check runs only at the top of each iteration, so a tick that reaches end on exactly the 1000th jump still falls into `else` and fails.",
    "failure_scenario": "999 timers due within the span plus the final jump to end. The loop finishes its last jump at end, never re-checks, and pytest.fail('more than 1000 timers came due') fires even though exactly 1000 jumps completed the tick."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/test_harness.py",
    "line": 730,
    "summary": "test_teardown_releases_faults_before_the_fixtures_and_after drives the hook generator by hand, so it never checks that pytest runs it before fixture finalizers. The 'after' release is never exercised by real pytest teardown.",
    "failure_scenario": "Remove tryfirst, or change the hook to run after teardown_exact (for example, move the release into the finally only). This test still passes, because it calls next()/send() itself. Only test_a_write_left_held_does_not_hang_teardown covers real ordering, and only the 'before' half."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/conftest.py",
    "line": 95,
    "summary": "The trailing release_all_faults() runs after every fixture finalizer, including the one that closes the event loop. Clearing a fault that a call is still waiting on then calls Event.set → future.set_result → loop.call_soon on a closed loop.",
    "failure_scenario": "A fixture finalizer installs a hang or drop_reply, and a call starts waiting on it before the loop closes. The post-teardown release_all_faults raises RuntimeError('Event loop is closed') from the hook, and pytest reports a teardown error for that test. The only case this 'after' release exists for is the one where it errors."
  }
]
```

