    "summary": "The `capabilities` knob makes `supports_native_users` True, but the mock still overrides `async_set_usercode`, `async_clear_usercode` and `async_get_usercodes`. In 'native-user' mode it therefore never runs the `_set_credential`, `async_get_users` or `_project_users_to_slots` paths that real Z-Wave and Matter providers take.",
    "failure_scenario": "The PR #1344 recovery and the #1482 setup run as a hybrid: native exchange budgets (3 per set, managed+1 per clear) and native capacity checks, but slot-only writes and reads. A regression in the native user lifecycle after revalidation would pass these tests. The knob also ties 'reports capabilities' to 'native users', so a slot-only lock that reports capabilities (as VirtualLock does) cannot be expressed. Its comment says capabilities are 'read on every ask', but BaseLock caches a successful probe for the instance's lifetime, so switching PIN_CAPABILITIES to NO_PIN_CAPABILITIES on a live lock is never seen."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/test_init.py",
    "line": 1114,
    "summary": "The `after_it_loads` parametrization of the PR #251 test cannot fail with the fix reverted, but it carries the same 'Regression for PR #251' docstring.",
    "failure_scenario": "Revert PR #251: only `before_it_loads` fails and `after_it_loads` stays green. The parametrized id suggests double coverage that does not exist, and that variant breaks the batch's rule that each test must fail with its fix reverted."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/test_services.py",
    "line": 281,
    "summary": "The PR #928 test builds its own deobfuscation input: it masks with `mask_pin(..., instance_id.async_get(hass))` and writes the log line format by hand. The text LCM actually logs is salted with `hass.data[DOMAIN]['instance_id']`.",
    "failure_scenario": "The shared data could be rebuilt with a different or empty salt after the last unload while the service still finds some salt. Then LCM's real log tokens (`BaseLock.mask_pin`) would stop matching, yet the test passes, because it hand-salts with HA's instance id instead of capturing a line the lock really logged."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/test_coordinator.py",
    "line": 574,
    "summary": "The #1268 test calls the integration's `async_get_config_entry_diagnostics` directly instead of going through Home Assistant's diagnostics HTTP endpoint, and the field it checks is a straight passthrough of `coordinator.last_update_success`.",
    "failure_scenario": "This asserts an internal coordinator flag through a thin wrapper. It skips the redaction and JSON serialization a downloaded report goes through, so a report that fails to serialize, or redacts or drops the field, would still pass. 'What a downloaded diagnostics report says' is not what is being tested."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/test_init.py",
    "line": 823,
    "summary": "The #865 and #247 tests (lines 823 and 656) simulate a restart by flipping an already running hass back to CoreState.starting. hass.data[DOMAIN] and everything async_setup created survive from the earlier load (#865 even loads and unloads both entries first), which a real restart never has.",
    "failure_scenario": "A real restart re-runs async_setup with empty hass.data (instance_id, resources, renames). If startup behavior ever depends on that first-time initialization, these tests carry stale state from the previous load and can pass where a real restart fails. HA also never goes running to starting, so any code that tracks state transitions sees an impossible sequence."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/test_init.py",
    "line": 1108,
    "summary": "The #1538 insurance test has no fix to revert, and its `kept.codes` assertion holds trivially: the mock's default table {1:'1234', 2:'5678'} is exactly the configured PINs.",
    "failure_scenario": "The codes check adds nothing beyond the call_order check. The test never confirms that the lock it watches is still the entry's instance after the options change (`mock_lock(entry, LOCK_1_ENTITY_ID) is kept`), so if the drop rebuilt the kept lock, writes to the new instance would go unseen. It cannot be checked under the batch's fix-reverted rule."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/test_harness.py",
    "line": 1330,
    "summary": "The current_entry harness test asserts `entry.version == CONFIG_ENTRY_VERSION` and `entry_users(entry)` equal to exactly what the constructor passed in, so both pass regardless. The 'without ... reading the lock' in its name is never asserted.",
    "failure_scenario": "Migration only runs when version < current, so the version check restates the input. If current_entry produced a shape that setup read differently (e.g. a minor_version or extra data key that triggers a migration that keeps the version), only `built == [mock_lock(entry)]` could catch it, and only if the migration builds a lock."
  }
]
