content.content:
Skill "code-review" completed (forked execution).

Result:
I ran the code checks in a scratch copy, so the worktree is unchanged. With the xfail marker turned off, the new test fails at its final assertion, `dropped.service_calls["setup"]` has one extra entry, which means it does reproduce the leak. The machine passes in both profiles: 15 examples in 1.2 s with `dev`, and 200 examples in 12.7 s with `ci`. Ten findings follow, most severe first.

```json
[
  {
    "file": "tests/properties/test_shared_lock_machine.py",
    "line": 882,
    "summary": "When a hang causes a failure, teardown cannot stop Home Assistant before the 10 s per-step watchdog fires. Home Assistant's own stop stages wait up to 20/100/60/30 s, so the instance is never removed from phcc's INSTANCES list and verify_cleanup aborts the whole pytest session.",
    "failure_scenario": "A product deadlock leaves a tracked task hung (the case the watchdog exists for). The step is cancelled after 10 s, `_async_unload_all` times out after another 10 s, and `hass.async_stop` sits in its 100 s STOP stage until the watchdog cancels it at 10 s. EVENT_HOMEASSISTANT_CLOSE never fires, so `clear_instance` never removes this hass from INSTANCES. At test end verify_cleanup sees two or more instances and calls `pytest.exit('Detected non stopped instances')`, so every later test goes unrun and no shrunk example is reported. Each failing example also costs about 30 s, so the shrink loop plus the final replay goes past the 420 s budget. After a cancelled stop, `loop.shutdown_default_executor()` is also unbounded (300 s default join)."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/properties/test_shared_lock_machine.py",
    "line": 917,
    "summary": "The 420 s timeout is not a hard bound. pytest-timeout's default signal method calls `pytest.fail`, and Hypothesis catches `_pytest.outcomes.Failed` as an ordinary failure of the current example. Separately, the per-step watchdog cancels only once.",
    "failure_scenario": "Generation plus a slow shrink pass 420 s. SIGALRM raises Failed inside the running example, Hypothesis records it as the failure and starts shrinking or replaying, and replays do not reproduce it because the alarm is one-shot. The result is a Flaky error or an unbounded run instead of a named timeout. Separately, a step that swallows its one `task.cancel()` (for example, code that catches CancelledError and keeps waiting) is never cancelled again, and `run_until_complete` blocks until that same unreliable alarm."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/properties/test_shared_lock_machine.py",
    "line": 719,
    "summary": "The documented leak skip is broader than the leak: after any NO_PUSH failure, `reload_the_locks_integration` is disabled for the rest of the example, even though only the dropped instance is affected.",
    "failure_scenario": "NO_PUSH is drawn early in an example. From then on no step reloads the lock integration, so for the remaining (up to 19) steps the machine never checks that live instances set up again after a reload, or that unloaded or PROVIDER_BUG-dropped instances do not. A sibling-entry regression that only shows after a failed push setup and a lock-integration reload is hidden. A narrower fix: keep the rule, and exempt only the ids in `dropped_without_push` from the setup-again check, naming the xfail."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/properties/test_shared_lock_machine.py",
    "line": 837,
    "summary": "`torn_down_instances_never_set_up_again` records an instance's setup count the first time an invariant sees it torn down, which is after the whole step. A setup that re-runs within the same step that tore the instance down is folded into that baseline and never flagged.",
    "failure_scenario": "During one entry reload (or a lock-integration reload) the old instance is unloaded, and a leaked listener re-runs its `async_setup` before the step settles. The baseline already includes the extra setup, so the invariant passes. `one_live_instance_per_held_lock` ignores it too, because the instance has an unload record. The mock's `call_order` already shows a 'setup' after 'unload' and would catch this exactly."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/test_init.py",
    "line": 1232,
    "summary": "The strict xfail has no `raises=` and its precondition asserts sit inside the same test, so any exception counts as the expected failure.",
    "failure_scenario": "A later change makes setup produce two instances (`[dropped] = built` raises ValueError), or leaves the entry not LOADED, or changes what setup records. The test still reports xfail even though it no longer proves the leak, and strict mode never notices. If the leak is fixed while an earlier assert breaks, the xfail stays green instead of XPASS-failing."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/properties/test_shared_lock_machine.py",
    "line": 605,
    "summary": "`boot_together` builds its own input: it sets an already running Home Assistant (which may have loaded entries and live lock instances) back to CoreState.starting, then fires EVENT_HOMEASSISTANT_STARTED a second time. Real Home Assistant never reaches that state.",
    "failure_scenario": "One entry is loaded and holding a lock when `boot_together` runs for the other two. Production never has live entries during a startup, nor a second STARTED event. A failure that only appears in this mix is a false positive, and boot behavior that depends on nothing else running yet is never exercised as it is in production. The entries are also set up one after another, not concurrently the way Home Assistant does at boot."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/common.py",
    "line": 1673,
    "summary": "`async_setup_mock_lock_entry` creates the two calendar entities once and hands the same objects to every calendar platform setup. The machine's reload rule and the new xfail test now reload this entry, and on each reload the calendars fail to re-add and disappear.",
    "failure_scenario": "`async_reload(lock_entry)` logs `HomeAssistantError: Entity 'calendar.test_1' cannot be added a second time to an entity platform` (seen when the xfail test runs with `--runxfail`). After that the calendar entities are gone and `hass.data['lock_code_manager_calendars']` holds removed objects. Any later step or test that uses those calendars as condition entities sees no entity, and every reload step adds ERROR log noise."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/properties/test_shared_lock_machine.py",
    "line": 222,
    "summary": "The frozen clock is active for the whole example, so freezegun also patches Hypothesis's own timing (`hypothesis.stateful.perf_counter`). Draw and step timings are corrupted, and its slow-test health checks see zero draw time.",
    "failure_scenario": "`--hypothesis-show-statistics` reports 'Typical runtimes: ~ 25-1765072231280 ms' (a frozen epoch minus real time). Draw times measured on the frozen clock are always 0, so `HealthCheck.too_slow` can never fire for this machine, and the per-rule and per-invariant runtime numbers used to diagnose slow examples are wrong."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/properties/test_shared_lock_machine.py",
    "line": 710,
    "summary": "`push_lock_reports_a_use` claims to check 'exactly once, however many instances listen'. In practice it can only ever fire one report: `push_subscriptions_balance` already limits listeners to one, and `listening` comes from the mock's own subscribe counters, not from a real subscription delivery path.",
    "failure_scenario": "A regression where a torn-down instance keeps receiving reports through a real callback, without a counted subscription, is never fired here, because the rule only replays the use on instances whose counters say they subscribed. In every reachable state that is exactly one call, so `uses == 1` reduces to 'one call fires one event' plus an owner-attribution check."
  },
  {
    "file": "AGENTS.md",
    "line": 416,
    "summary": "The new documentation says a failure 'shrinks in seconds, well inside the suite's 60-second test timeout', but the test raises its own timeout to 420 s because a shrink may not fit.",
    "failure_scenario": "A maintainer who trusts AGENTS.md removes or lowers the 420 s mark, or treats a run of several minutes as a hang. A real slow shrink is then cut off at 60 s and reported unshrunk, or as a timeout, which is the outcome the in-code comment warns about."
  }
]
```

