Result:
```json
[
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/config_flow.py",
    "line": 460,
    "summary": "On the new direct path, the yaml step creates the entry while the flow's cur_step is still the yaml FORM. The duplicate-request guard in `_take_allocation` therefore no longer protects entry creation: a second request re-runs the whole step from scratch instead of getting UnknownFlow.",
    "failure_scenario": "Matter-only entry on the yaml path. Request A submits the users. The task finishes eagerly, so `yaml_allocated` clears `_allocation_task`/`_allocation_submission` and returns create_entry, and HA then awaits `async_finish_flow` (adding the entry and setting it up). During that await HA has not updated `flow.cur_step`, so it is still the yaml form with its data_schema. Request B (a client retry or a second dialog, the case `test_a_second_request_for_an_answered_step_is_not_an_unknown_error` exists for) passes schema validation. In `async_step_yaml`, `_allocation_progress` returns None because the task was already taken, so B validates again, starts a new allocation that also finishes eagerly, and reaches `_async_create_entry` a second time. HA's `async_finish_flow` then finds an existing entry with the same unique_id, unloads it and creates a replacement. Before this change the flow was parked on progress/progress_done and B hit `_take_allocation` and raised UnknownFlow."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/providers/matter/test_e2e.py",
    "line": 372,
    "summary": "The Matter e2e tests do not pin the property the fix depends on (\"Matter has nothing to read\"). Every Matter helper is an AsyncMock that never suspends, so allocation finishes eagerly whether or not it reads the Matter lock.",
    "failure_scenario": "Suppose a regression makes allocation read Matter locks: `credential_index_follows_slot` flips, or `async_check_slot_capacity`/`async_max_slot` calls `get_lock_info`/`get_lock_users`. In production those calls go to the Matter server and suspend, so progress is shown and #1639 comes back. In this test the patched AsyncMocks return synchronously, the task is still done when `_start_allocation` checks it, and both tests still go straight to code_slot / create_entry. The tests never assert that the allocation-time helpers (for example `get_lock_info`, `get_lock_users`) were not awaited before the entry was created. As written they pass under the very input contract the mocks supply."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/config_flow.py",
    "line": 460,
    "summary": "An exception stored on the finished task (CancelledError or another BaseException that `_allocate_for`'s `except Exception` does not catch) now surfaces in a different place than before. `task.result()` used to raise inside HA's detached done-callback `call_configure` task; it now raises inside the user's own submitting request.",
    "failure_scenario": "A provider path raises asyncio.CancelledError synchronously during eager start (for example, the client connection is torn down). Before: the task ended cancelled, HA's `schedule_configure` re-entered the step, `_take_allocation`'s `task.result()` raised inside a background task, and the flow stayed on progress_done. Now: `_take_allocation` raises CancelledError out of `async_step_ui`/`async_step_yaml`, through `hass.config_entries.flow.async_configure`, into the HTTP/websocket handler. A CancelledError there looks like the request itself was cancelled, not like a 500. Meanwhile `_allocation_task`/`_allocation_submission` are already cleared and cur_step is unchanged, so the client sees an aborted request with no response."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/config_flow.py",
    "line": 460,
    "summary": "The `getattr(self, f\"async_step_{step_id}_allocated\")` dispatch skips HA's `_raise_if_step_does_not_exist` check, which the old `async_show_progress_done(next_step_id=...)` path went through. A missing sibling step now fails differently.",
    "failure_scenario": "Any caller of `_start_allocation` whose step_id has no `<step>_allocated` method (a new allocating step, or a renamed one) fails only when the task finishes eagerly. It raises AttributeError, which reaches the user as \"Unknown error\" and leaves the flow alive on its form. The progress path for the same caller would raise UnknownStep and remove the flow. The error therefore depends on lock timing, and in tests (where MockLCMLock reads never suspend) only the AttributeError path runs. No current caller is affected, since only \"ui\" and \"yaml\" are used and both have siblings."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/config_flow.py",
    "line": 478,
    "summary": "The `_take_allocation` docstring and the `async_configure_flow` test helper still describe the step as always parking on progress/progress_done. After this change, the progress-done handoff (and the UnknownFlow guard on it) is only reached when the read actually suspends.",
    "failure_scenario": "Because MockLCMLock reads (`_holding`) never suspend, almost every config-flow test that uses `async_configure_flow` now takes the direct path and never enters its SHOW_PROGRESS loop. Only three tests built around hang/Event still exercise HA's done-callback re-entry (`test_ui_allocation_runs_as_a_progress_step`, `test_reentering_the_step...`, `test_a_second_request...`). A regression in `_allocation_progress`/progress_done handling, for example on the yaml refusal path, would go undetected because nothing drives a refusal through progress anymore."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/providers/matter/test_e2e.py",
    "line": 335,
    "summary": "The new `_matter_helpers_patched` context manager re-implements the helper patching that the `lcm_entry` fixture in `tests/providers/matter/conftest.py` already does, as a hand-written list of seven `patch(f\"{_PROVIDER_MODULE}.<name>\", ...)` calls.",
    "failure_scenario": "There are now two places that decide which Matter helpers get patched for an end-to-end setup: a hard-coded list in the conftest fixture and a dict-driven loop in test_e2e. Adding a helper to the provider requires remembering both, or one path makes a real call. A shared context manager in conftest, used by both the `lcm_entry` fixture and this class, would remove the duplication."
  }
]
```

